WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:01.689
Hello there!

00:00:01.689 --> 00:00:09.570
Ms. Coffee Bean is here to explain PET,
no the other PET... no, not this one Ms. Coffee

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:13.129
Bean,
obviously I mean the pattern-exploiting training

00:00:13.129 --> 00:00:14.290
kind of PET.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:17.260
Yeah, who would have guessed it?

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:23.690
It sounds a little complicated but it shows
that any language model, not only a huge one

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:30.250
like GPT-3 is able to learn new tasks from
few training examples.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:36.980
And this video will explain all about PET
and its sisters iPET and ADAPET, meaning that

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:42.600
in a couple of minutes, you will see how it
is possible for a relatively small language

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:56.190
model, with only a fraction of the size of
GPT-3 to learn a new task from only 32 examples.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:01:02.820
That large language models like GPT-3 can
do well on new tasks from really few examples,

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:07.360
has been known since ages ago, in Machine
Learning time…

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.440
although in my normal time, it feels like
it was yesterday.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:17.880
It's important to recapitulate how GPT-3 does
few-shot learning, before explaining the PET

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.520
alternative for much smaller models.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:27.420
With only 32 labelled examples, GPT-3 achieves
almost state of the art results for some SuperGLUE

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.420
tasks.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:32.620
SuperGLUE is THE benchmark for natural language
understanding.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:36.290
And GPT-3 does it with priming.

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:41.830
Priming means that the models get as input
this whole thing here at once.

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:48.240
This contains first a task description, then
the few labelled examples you have (it’s

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.560
few shot learning, so you should have a few
labelled examples) and then the example you

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:56.150
want GPT-3 to label.

00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:58.520
But why would THIS ever work?

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:05.610
Well, we remember that GPT-3 is transformer-based
– obviously, and between these tokens there

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:11.920
is self-attention computing the pattern, figuring
out what the next token, so the label, should

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:16.840
be, given all these previous examples as context.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:21.660
And supposing you have a model like GPT-3
which has already seen virtually the whole

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:27.250
internet, there is a high chance that the
model would pick up the pattern and predict

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:28.460
the right thing.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:33.780
But the first drawback of this method is that
you usually do not have GPT-3 lying around

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:34.890
to help you.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:40.900
The second drawback is the limited number
of examples you can give to GPT-3, because

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:46.400
this whole input is parsed into tokens and
unfortunately, the number of tokens cannot

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.370
exceed a maximum number.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:53.710
So the number of examples you can give was
defined already when programming the model

00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:58.490
and is constrained by memory and the quadratically
scaling attention.

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:05.050
But do not worry: PET is here to turn the
situation upside down, yeah quite literally,

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:12.860
and promises few-shot learning for normal-sized
models, like BERT or ALBERT… wait.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:14.960
Since when is BERT normal-sized!?

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:21.240
Anyway, so you want BERT to be able to solve
a certain task, but you have only a small

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:23.250
bunch of annotated data.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:29.220
So you decide to apply PET and for this, you
need two more ingredients other than few labels:

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:32.819
the so-called pattern and the verbalizer.

00:03:32.819 --> 00:03:37.250
For example, if you want to do few-shot learning
on question answering, you should have a few

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:41.280
pairs of questions and answers lying around,
then you are ready

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:42.750
to create patterns.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:46.880
And not only one, but as many as you can come
up with!

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:53.680
The pattern is a pre-defined template, a so-called
cloze style question, leaving blank space

00:03:53.680 --> 00:04:00.410
for inserting the answer or an equivalent
token bearing the same meaning as the answer.

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:02.730
So let’s see an example for one of the patterns.

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:07.640
Keep in mind that the blank should be predicted
later by the model from few examples.

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:14.470
Now the verbalizer comes in: The verbalizer
translates the actual answer of the problem

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:19.579
to a token that has the same meaning as the
actual answer.

00:04:19.579 --> 00:04:24.750
It is quite hard to wrap Ms. Coffee Bean’s
head around this, so let’s discuss a little.

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:29.710
One reason for employing the verbalizer is
to reduce the problem complexity, because

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:35.820
various verbalizations like “sure”, “definitely”,
are actually the same thing as saying “yes”.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:41.280
And just “yes” instead of infinite variations
of it, is easier to understand for the model,

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:47.010
especially in few-shot learning where it has
only few examples to learn from.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:52.360
Ms. Coffee Bean's guess is that this verbalizer
complicates the paper a bit because of a choice

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:57.310
the authors made in the beginning: If you
constrain the patterns to have the blank at

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:03.310
the end, you can use a GPT-like autoregressive
transformers to predict as many tokens from

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:05.940
left to right as you want to fill in the blank.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:10.580
But what if you can only think of patterns
with the blank in the middle?

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:15.650
Well, for this case, the authors designed
PET to work with masked language models like

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:21.770
BERT that are exactly designed for inserting
missing things everywhere in the sentence,

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:24.510
not only at the end like GPT.

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:30.250
This makes it possible to constrain the pattern
much less and you can mask out things everywhere,

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:31.960
which is much more flexible.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:37.350
In the vanilla PET, this space you leave out
can be only one token long as this is the

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.970
natural set-up for BERT-like models.

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:44.949
In the follow-up paper, PET also allows for
multiple masked tokens to be predicted one

00:05:44.949 --> 00:05:46.680
by one, from left to right.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:52.940
But vanilla PET did not allow for multiple
tokens first, therefore this verbalizer comes

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:58.990
in as a function that maps more complex outputs
or answers to single label tokens, in a way

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:03.300
that simplifies the many variants the answer
can have.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:05.419
Okay, so let’s recapitulate.

00:06:05.419 --> 00:06:10.280
You have some few labelled instances for a
task that a language model like BERT has not

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:15.480
seen so far, so you decide to teach the model
to solve the task with PET.

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:20.780
Therefore you create patterns with gaps where
the model should hopefully insert the right

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:24.020
answer and fill the patterns with your examples.

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:28.160
You have more patterns and you can’t decide
which pattern to take?

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:33.050
Well for PET, the more the better: take all
of them and go on with the PET algorithm by

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:38.580
training your masked language model for each
pattern on all examples.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:43.070
So if you have 3 patterns, you should train
at least 3 language models.

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:48.640
But in practice you will end up with something
like 9, since training a language models on

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:52.960
so little data as 32 examples is very unstable.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:58.080
So yeah, you should train more than one model
per pattern, but it does not take long on

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:02.710
so few examples, therefore this ensembling
procedure is feasible.

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:09.180
Then the PET recipe says, you should use this
ensemble of language models as teachers to

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:16.570
annotate a set of unlabeled examples and use
these silver labels to train a regular sequence

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:22.440
classifier which should be your final model
for making predictions on the new task.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:25.650
Hm… what was this training setting called?

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:30.490
Ah yes, distillation, yes it's distillation
indeed!

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:35.800
And if you do this iteratively, gradually
increasing the size of the unlabeled dataset

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:40.300
based on the labels of the previous iteration,
you have iPET!

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:45.919
By doing this training and automatic annotating
procedure iteratively, you hope to gradually

00:07:45.919 --> 00:07:50.650
increase the quality of the teacher models
and your silver data.

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:56.240
What if in some iteration everything deviates
and you end up generating nonsense in the

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:57.240
end?

00:07:57.240 --> 00:07:58.240
Hmm…

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:02.110
Well, in any case:
So the gist of step 2 is to make gradient

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:07.009
updates, but because you do this on very little
data, the training is unstable.

00:08:07.009 --> 00:08:11.860
Therefore you need an ensemble of these unstable
models from which you distill, but for distillation

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:15.229
you also have to gather unlabeled data somehow.

00:08:15.229 --> 00:08:20.550
But does one always have task-specific unlabeled
data lying around?

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:26.639
No, therefore different authors propose a
follow-up on PET called ADAPET.

00:08:26.639 --> 00:08:30.570
ADAPET takes the distillation steps away,
but how?

00:08:30.570 --> 00:08:37.079
Why should a language model ever converge
with unstable fine-tuning on just 32 examples?

00:08:37.079 --> 00:08:40.469
Well, by introducing more fine-grained losses!

00:08:40.469 --> 00:08:46.550
In PET the language model is allowed only
to consider task label tokens for the blank

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:52.089
space, meaning that there will be gradient
updates only for the label tokens.

00:08:52.089 --> 00:08:59.029
So if we would list all the words the language
model knows, then only the labels, like "yes"

00:08:59.029 --> 00:09:04.449
or "no" would be adapted to the new task,
but not the representations for other words

00:09:04.449 --> 00:09:05.800
like “calendar”.

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:12.660
To alleviate of this, ADAPET considers every
word or token of the vocabulary for the blank.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:19.189
It reinforces the correct label and pushes
the other words further away, adapting representations

00:09:19.189 --> 00:09:22.050
of all words in the vocabulary.

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:26.399
Ms. Coffee Bean’s intuition behind this
would be that if one wants to do few-shot

00:09:26.399 --> 00:09:31.950
learning with gradient updates and no distillation,
the model should be given more chances to

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:33.410
adapt for the new task.

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:38.369
She does not think it would be enough to change
only the representations for the class tokens

00:09:38.369 --> 00:09:43.279
and hope that the dynamic representation computation
by attention does all the rest.

00:09:43.279 --> 00:09:48.720
At least not when one does not start with
GPT-3 as a base model in the first place,

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:53.879
because GPT-3 does not even need any gradient
updates for few-shot learning.

00:09:53.879 --> 00:09:58.389
Therefore it seems like a good solution to
engineer many ways for the model to make the

00:09:58.389 --> 00:09:59.600
right changes.

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:04.619
Question: in which cases would it make perhaps
the wrong changes?

00:10:04.619 --> 00:10:09.269
Ms. Coffee Bean’s assumption is in cases
where the original training of the base model,

00:10:09.269 --> 00:10:13.819
for example BERT, is very far away from the
task one needs to learn.

00:10:13.819 --> 00:10:19.371
She would not expect PET to enable few-shot
learning for HTML code classification, if

00:10:19.371 --> 00:10:22.689
the base model had never seen HTML code before.

00:10:22.689 --> 00:10:28.959
In contrast to HTML code, SuperGLUE is relatively
close to what BERT has seen already.

00:10:28.959 --> 00:10:34.500
In any case, another approach is to give the
model as much information as you can, so to

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:39.550
give the model the same few examples you already
have, but from a different perspective.

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:46.839
And that is exactly ADAPET’s second addition
to PET: it adds one more task (and loss) in

00:10:46.839 --> 00:10:48.089
a multi-task setting.

00:10:48.089 --> 00:10:53.759
It optimizes not only for the answer, but
also for predicting the right context given

00:10:53.759 --> 00:10:55.249
the correct answer.

00:10:55.249 --> 00:10:59.500
This is valuable, especially for few-shot
learning, to give the model the chance to

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:02.329
learn as much about the task as possible.

00:11:02.329 --> 00:11:07.149
While PET tried to tackle few-shot learning
by distillation, in other words by automatically

00:11:07.149 --> 00:11:13.100
annotating unlabeled data and the learning
from it, ADAPET adds refined and more detailed

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:15.869
and informative losses instead of distillation.

00:11:15.869 --> 00:11:21.179
Cool that was it, now you know about PET,
iPET and ADAPET!

00:11:21.179 --> 00:11:25.889
Check out the experiments in the papers, where
you basically see how PET and its variations

00:11:25.889 --> 00:11:30.689
can make ALBERT… wait, who is ALBERT.

00:11:30.689 --> 00:11:33.339
Ms. Coffee Bean, do you have anything to confess?

00:11:33.339 --> 00:11:36.920
No, ALBERT is just another BERT variant.

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:42.970
So, I was saying that you will see PET applied
on ALBERT, which is a much smaller model than

00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:49.319
GPT-3 and can also lead to impressive few-shot
results on SuperGLUE after seeing just 32

00:11:49.319 --> 00:11:50.430
labelled examples.

00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:52.980
For more on this, check out the papers linked
below!

00:11:52.980 --> 00:11:57.809
So to wrap up, here is the take-home message
from Ms. Coffee Bean: PET enables few-shot

00:11:57.809 --> 00:12:03.619
learning by distillation from an ensemble
of models trained on patterns capturing the

00:12:03.619 --> 00:12:08.249
task as a cloze task, which is just a fancy
way of saying “fill in the gaps”.

00:12:08.249 --> 00:12:14.110
iPET enables the patterns to capture more
tokens than just only one and then ADAPET

00:12:14.110 --> 00:12:19.970
simplifies pet by using patterns, but replacing
distillation with better losses.

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:25.809
I hope you see the pattern here, where one
important step in few-shot learning, has become

00:12:25.809 --> 00:12:28.500
to engineer patterns or prompts.

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:33.519
Very much like when priming GPT-3, you still
have to think a little about framing your

00:12:33.519 --> 00:12:35.489
task to present it to the model.

00:12:35.489 --> 00:12:41.230
So, no wonder a new job description was created,
called prompt engineering!

00:12:41.230 --> 00:12:46.009
But the difference between GPT-3 few-shot
learning and PET-enabled few-shot learning

00:12:46.009 --> 00:12:51.730
is the following: When priming GPT-3, you
do not need further training or gradient updates.

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:58.809
I honestly do not know why it is called few-shot
LEARNING, because there is no learning anymore,

00:12:58.809 --> 00:13:05.670
just dynamic computation of a new label given
the existing examples as context.

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:10.809
With a model that has seen the whole internet
already, it is ok to expect this to work.

00:13:10.809 --> 00:13:16.959
GPT-3’s few-shot application broadness comes
in Ms. Coffee Bean’s humble opinion from

00:13:16.959 --> 00:13:22.329
the variation of data it has already seen
and we do not think anything trained on less

00:13:22.329 --> 00:13:25.819
data variance can compete with that aspect.

00:13:25.819 --> 00:13:32.699
But with PET, one does indeed few-shot learning,
because it needs little training, gradient

00:13:32.699 --> 00:13:39.779
updates, LEARNING, and it enables few-shot
learning with much smaller models than GPT-3

00:13:39.779 --> 00:13:43.470
that did not see the same amount of data variation.

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:48.639
But one question remains:
How far can PET go, apart from the SuperGLUE

00:13:48.639 --> 00:13:49.639
benchmark?

00:13:49.639 --> 00:13:50.569
Hihi, okay, bye!

